# Line-ending policy.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, and this repository had no
# .gitattributes, so line endings were decided by each contributor's local Git
# config rather than by the project.
#
# The visible symptom was in the generated snapshot: Git checked static-site/
# out with CRLF, `next build` rewrote it with LF, and `git status` then reported
# hundreds of modified files that contained no actual change — 452 of them in
# one measured case, with `git diff` showing nothing for any of them. That noise
# hides real edits and would make the drift check proposed in UPD-005 fail on
# any Windows checkout.
#
# Normalize text to LF in the repository, and check it out as LF everywhere.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The generated snapshot must round-trip byte-for-byte against a fresh build,
# which always writes LF.
static-site/** text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF; bash rejects CRLF line endings, which would break
# `npm run sync:static` on a Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary assets Git should never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
